{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310793,"nonce":"7Ed6U7G5XqieWX6FSFs7XfZ4/qM=","title":"OPEN 2026-07-05: pure-Haxe Q4 matmul path = 2.9-4.7 tok/s vs FFI kernels 45.9 tok/s IN-PROCESS (same binary, --no-cache) — ~15x ...","message":"Honest same-binary numbers (llama-chat 1B, default run = LLVM tier now, --no-cache):\n- FFI kernels (no RAYZOR_HAXE_MATMUL): **45.9 tok/s** (short run)\n- Pure-Haxe matmul (RAYZOR_HAXE_MATMUL=1): **2.9-4.7 tok/s** (64-token sustained, pool on)\n- Pool A/B verified: pool ON 4.73/4.39 vs workers=1 2.10 tok/s = **2.2x**; user/real CPU ratio\n  confirms engagement. RAYZOR_HAXE_MATMUL_WORKERS=1 is the off-knob.\n- Decode step p50 164ms ≈ 17 GFLOP/s — ~10-20x under SDOT kernel capability ⇒ overhead-bound,\n  not bandwidth/compute-bound.\n\n- \"~73 tok/s (8w) at/above FFI parity\" ([[bugs_nue_metaint_unbox_raw_enum_payload]] Phase-4\n  note, 5feb4802): predates the dtype-aware setFlat correctness fix (21d13b77). The fast path\n  then wrote y via raw Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e (f64 stride) which CORRUPTS F32 buffers — the 73 number was\n  measured with the corrupting fast writes and/or different output dtype. Don't cite as parity.\n- \"cranelift ≈ LLVM parity 3.3 vs 3.5\" (this window): true, but BOTH are the slow kernel.\n\n1. **y.setFlat(idx, v) per output element** (rows × batch per matmul; ~30k+/token): dtype\n   switch + call overhead per element. Fix direction: typed F32 pointer writes (a real\n   Ptr\u003cF32\u003e/typed store, or a row-batched write API), NOT raw Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e (f64 corrupts F32 —\n   that's why setFlat exists).\n2. **x.getFlat per element in quantizeQ8K** (2×K per row) — same family.\n3. Verify SDOT actually fires in the LLVM-tier JIT'd q4DotMA4 by DISASSEMBLY (feedback rule);\n   verified historically for isolated benches, never re-verified inside the model at HEAD.\n\n- 47b0619c batch-inner matmul: weights read once per output row regardless of batch (prefill\n  was seq × decode cost; profiled 157ms/token prefill = same as decode). Bit-exact (per-(n,r)\n  block accumulation order unchanged). Prefill profile after: 128ms/tok — modest because the\n  kernel is overhead-bound, not bandwidth-bound; the restructure pays off once #1/#2 are fixed.\n- RAYZOR_PROFILE_DECODE=1 gives prefill_fwd_s / step_p50_ms split (GenerationLoop).\n\n- Editing nue/*.hx (imported modules) WITHOUT --no-cache → stale-MIR mix → exit 132 trap at\n  GGUF load. Looked like a --release/FFI regression; it wasn't. ALWAYS --no-cache after edits.\n- Benchmarks without --release under-measure? --release run was stale-cache-crashing; with\n  --no-cache it works; re-A/B --release vs not before attributing.\n- llama-chat rayzor.toml [tier] overrides presets silently — check it before any tier claim.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_haxe_q4_kernel_overhead_gap` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}